# Code Comments

Comments explain **why** and **non-obvious behavior** — not what the code literally does. Good code should be self-explanatory for simple operations.

## When Comments Are Required

| Location | Requirement |
|----------|-------------|
| Exported service functions | JSDoc with `@param`, `@returns`, and brief purpose |
| Provider classes | Class-level JSDoc; JSDoc on public interface methods |
| Complex business logic | Inline comment for non-obvious steps (race conditions, provider quirks) |
| Enums / config schemas | Brief file-level or export comment when values aren't self-evident |
| Middleware | JSDoc explaining what context vars are set and when |
| Metrics modules | File-level comment listing what is tracked |

## JSDoc Pattern (Services & Providers)

```typescript
/**
 * Exchange an authorization code for OAuth tokens.
 * @param code - Authorization code from the provider callback
 * @returns Token response including access_token and optional refresh_token
 */
async exchangeCodeForToken(code: string): Promise<OAuthTokenResponse> {
```

```typescript
/**
 * Creates or updates a user by provider account ID.
 * Must run inside a transaction to prevent duplicate users on concurrent callbacks.
 * @param payload - User fields from the OAuth provider
 * @param options.tx - Required database transaction
 */
export async function upsertByProviderAccountId(payload: NewUser, options?: { tx?: DBTransaction })
```

Use `@throws` when the function throws domain-specific errors callers must handle.

## Inline Comments (Sparingly)

Use for business rules that aren't obvious from names:

```typescript
// Google doesn't always return a new refresh token on refresh — keep the old one if missing
return tokenData;

// Handle missing name fields from providers that omit given_name/family_name
const firstName = userInfo.given_name || userInfo.name?.split(" ")[0] || "User";
```

## When NOT to Comment

```typescript
// BAD — restates the code
// Get the user from the database
const user = await UsersService.findById(id);

// BAD — outdated or obvious
// TODO: fix this later
const result = await fetch(url);
```

Do not add comments on every line, every handler, or every variable.

## Handlers vs Services

- **Handlers:** OpenAPI `summary` / `description` / `.openapi()` metadata replaces most comments. Add inline comments only for tricky HTTP behavior (cookies, redirects, status edge cases).
- **Services & providers:** JSDoc on exported functions is required.
- **DB services:** JSDoc on each exported namespace function (match `UsersService` style in `packages/db`).

## Types & Interfaces

Public types in `apps/api/src/types.ts` and shared packages should have JSDoc when they define contracts others consume:

```typescript
/** Application-wide Hono bindings populated by getUserMiddleware */
export interface AppBindings { ... }
```

## Keep Comments Accurate

Update or remove comments when behavior changes. Stale comments are worse than none.
